-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 - Chapter 4A, RIGHT OF PRIVATE DEFENCE (ss 96-106A).
--
-- Source: Penal Code 1871, 2020 Revised Edition, SSO "Current version as at
-- 09 Sep 2026" (inputs/PC1871.txt, lines 3015-3304).
--
-- Chapter 4 is pc-exceptions-general.l4, whose header states the two
-- conventions this module shares and which a reader must know first:
--   * no offence here, so no `offence under s N` / `charge under s N`; s 96 is
--     exported as `exception under s 96` f and read beside the charge;
--   * POLARITY (FORK E-1): every leaf is phrased so that TRUE favours the
--     defender, and no ladder here puts NOT over a leaf, so that the charge
--     generator's FALSE-fill of an unknown leaf withholds the right - which is
--     Evidence Act 1893 s 107's "the court is to presume the absence of such
--     circumstances". The restrictions of ss 98 and 106A, which the Code words
--     as denials of the right, are therefore leaves in the defender's words
--     ("no reasonable opportunity to have recourse ...", "not done by a public
--     servant ..."), and their @desc says which way to answer.
--
-- One facts record, `Private Defence Facts`, for the whole Chapter: ss 97-106A
-- are one right, stated in parts, and one act of defence is judged against all
-- of them. The parts are separate exported ladders over that record, so each
-- draws as one box on `exception under s 96` and opens as its own ladder
-- (the charge generator's definitionFns).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pc-domain`
IMPORT `pc-general`

§ `Chapter 4A - Right of Private Defence`

§§ `The facts of one act of defence`

-- "Offence" in Chapter 4A means a thing punishable under this Code or any other
-- written law (s 40(2)).
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 96-106A
DECLARE `Private Defence Facts` HAS
    -- s 96
    `the act was done in defence against the act giving rise to the right`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the accused's act done to defend against the act of the assailant or wrongdoer described below?

    -- ss 97, 99: what the act defended against was
    `the act defended against is an offence`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act defended against an offence - punishable under this Code or any other written law (s 40(2))?
    `the act would be that offence but for the youth, want of maturity of understanding, unsoundness of mind or intoxication of the person doing it, or any misconception on his part`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the act defended against NOT that offence only because of the youth, want of maturity of understanding, unsoundness of mind or intoxication of the person doing it, or a misconception on his part (s 99)? For example, an attack by a madman (s 99 illus (a)), or by a householder who in good faith takes a lawful entrant for a housebreaker (s 99 illus (b)).
    `the act falls under the definition of an offence affecting the human body, or is an attempt or a threat to commit one`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act defended against fall under the definition of an offence affecting the human body (Chapter 16), or was it an attempt or a threat to commit one (ss 97(a), 101(1))?
    `the act falls under the definition of theft, or is an attempt to commit theft`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act defended against fall under the definition of theft (s 378), or was it an attempt to commit theft (ss 97(b), 104(1))?
    `the act falls under the definition of robbery, or is an attempt to commit robbery`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act defended against fall under the definition of robbery (s 390), or was it an attempt to commit robbery?
    `the act falls under the definition of mischief, or is an attempt to commit mischief`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act defended against fall under the definition of mischief (s 425), or was it an attempt to commit mischief?
    `the act falls under the definition of criminal trespass, or is an attempt to commit criminal trespass`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act defended against fall under the definition of criminal trespass (s 441) - which includes house-breaking (s 442) - or was it an attempt to commit criminal trespass?
    `the criminal trespass is house-breaking`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the criminal trespass house-breaking (s 442)?

    -- s 101: start and continuance of the right of private defence of the body
    `the defender reasonably believed that there was danger to the body, his own or that of any other person, arising from that act`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defender reasonably believe that there was danger to the body - his own or anyone else's - arising from that act, though the offence may not have been committed (s 101(1))?
    `that belief of danger continued when the act of defence was done`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did that belief of danger still continue when the defender did the act (s 101(2))?

    -- s 104(1): start of the right of private defence of property
    `the defender reasonably believed that there was a danger to property, his own or that of any other person, arising from that act`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defender reasonably believe that there was a danger to property - his own or anyone else's, movable or immovable (s 97(b)) - arising from that act (s 104(1))?
    -- s 104(2)-(5): continuance, by the offence defended against
    `the offender had not effected his retreat with the property`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Theft: when the act of defence was done, had the offender NOT yet got away with the property (s 104(2)(a))?
    `the assistance of a public authority had not been obtained`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Theft: had the assistance of a public authority NOT yet been obtained (s 104(2)(b))?
    `the property had not been recovered`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Theft: had the property NOT yet been recovered (s 104(2)(c))?
    `the offender was causing or attempting to cause to any person death or hurt or wrongful restraint`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Robbery: when the act of defence was done, was the offender causing, or attempting to cause, death, hurt or wrongful restraint to anyone (s 104(3))?
    `the fear of instant death or of instant hurt or of instant personal restraint continued`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Robbery: did the fear of instant death, instant hurt or instant personal restraint still continue (s 104(3))?
    `the offender continued in the commission of the criminal trespass or mischief`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Criminal trespass or mischief: was the offender still committing it when the act of defence was done (s 104(4))?
    `the house-breaking continued`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc House-breaking: was the house-breaking still continuing when the act of defence was done (s 104(5))?

    -- ss 102, 103: how far the right of private defence of the body extends
    `the harm voluntarily caused to the assailant was harm other than death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the harm the defender voluntarily caused to the assailant something other than death (s 103)?
    `an assault where the defender reasonably believes that death will otherwise be the consequence of such assault`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it an assault which the defender reasonably believed would otherwise cause death (s 102(a))?
    `an assault where the defender reasonably believes that grievous hurt will otherwise be the consequence of such assault`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it an assault which the defender reasonably believed would otherwise cause grievous hurt (s 102(b); s 320)?
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of committing rape as described in section 375 or causing such rape to be committed`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it an assault the defender reasonably believed was done with intent to commit rape as described in s 375, or to cause such rape to be committed (s 102(c))?
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of causing penile penetration of the vagina, anus or mouth as described in section 376(2)`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it an assault the defender reasonably believed was done with intent to cause penile penetration of the vagina, anus or mouth as described in s 376(2) (s 102(d))?
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of kidnapping or abducting`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it an assault the defender reasonably believed was done with intent to kidnap or abduct (s 102(e))?
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of wrongfully confining a person, under circumstances which may reasonably cause him to believe that he will be unable to have opportunity for recourse to a public authority for his release`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it an assault the defender reasonably believed was done with intent to wrongfully confine a person, in circumstances that could reasonably make him believe he would have no chance of recourse to a public authority for release (s 102(f))?

    -- s 100: risk of harm to an innocent person
    `the harm was caused to an innocent person, in running a risk of harm to an innocent person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defender's act harm an innocent person (not the assailant), in running a risk of harming innocent persons (s 100)?
    `an assault which causes the defender to reasonably believe that death would be caused to him or to any other person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the right being exercised against an assault which made the defender reasonably believe that death would be caused to him or to anyone else (s 100)?
    `the defender is so situated that he cannot effectually exercise that right without risk of harm to an innocent person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the defender so placed that he could not effectually exercise the right without risk of harm to an innocent person (s 100)?

    -- ss 105, 106: how far the right of private defence of property extends
    `the harm voluntarily caused to the wrongdoer was harm other than death`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the harm the defender voluntarily caused to the wrongdoer something other than death (s 106)?
    `the house-breaking was committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m.`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the house-breaking committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m. (s 105(1)(b))? Decide it with `after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m.`.
    `the wrongdoer effected his entrance into the house or any part of it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the danger to property arising from the wrongdoer's entering the house or any part of it (s 105(1)(b)(i))?
    `the wrongdoer, being in the house for the purpose of committing an offence or having committed an offence therein, leaves the house or any part of it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the wrongdoer, being in the house to commit an offence or having committed one there, leaving the house or any part of it (s 105(1)(b)(ii))?
    `enters or leaves through a passage made by the wrongdoer, or by any abettor of the house-breaking, in order to commit the house-breaking`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he enter or leave through a passage made by himself or an abettor in order to commit the house-breaking (s 105(2)(a))?
    `enters or leaves through a passage not intended for human entrance, or one reached by scaling or climbing over any wall or building`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he enter or leave through a passage not intended by anyone (other than himself or an abettor) for human entrance, or through a passage he reached by scaling or climbing over a wall or building (s 105(2)(b))?
    `enters or leaves through a passage opened, in order to commit the house-breaking, by means the occupier did not intend it to be opened by`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he enter or leave through a passage which he or an abettor had opened, in order to commit the house-breaking, by a means by which the occupier did not intend it to be opened (s 105(2)(c))?
    `enters or leaves by opening any lock in order to commit the house-breaking, or to leave after it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he enter or leave by opening a lock in order to commit the house-breaking, or in order to leave after it (s 105(2)(d))?
    `effects his entry or leaving by using criminal force or committing an assault, or by threatening any person with assault`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he enter or leave by using criminal force, committing an assault, or threatening anyone with assault (s 105(2)(e))?
    `enters or leaves by a passage he knows to have been fastened against it, and to have been unfastened by himself or an abettor`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he enter or leave by a passage he knew had been fastened against such entry or leaving and had been unfastened by himself or an abettor (s 105(2)(f))?
    `the mischief is mischief by fire committed on a building, tent, container or vessel used as a human dwelling, or as a place for the custody of property`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the mischief mischief by fire committed on a building, tent, container or vessel used as a human dwelling or as a place for the custody of property (s 105(1)(c))?
    `the defender reasonably believes that death or grievous hurt will be the consequence, if such right of private defence is not exercised`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc In a case of theft, mischief or house-breaking, did the defender reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would follow if he did not exercise the right (s 105(1)(d))?

    -- s 98: the restrictions
    `the harm inflicted was no more than it was reasonably necessary to inflict in the circumstances`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the harm the defender inflicted no more than was reasonably necessary in the circumstances (s 98(1))?
    `there was no reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public authority in the circumstances`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was there NO reasonable opportunity, in the circumstances, to have recourse to the protection of a public authority (s 98(2))? The right ceases the moment such an opportunity arises - for example, when a police patrol arrives in the midst of the struggle (s 98 illus (b)).

    -- s 106A: acts against which there is no right of private defence
    `the act causes the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the act defended against cause the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result (s 106A(1), (2))?
    `the act is not done or attempted by a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the act was NOT done or attempted by a public servant (s 21) acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office (s 106A(1))? Answer FALSE if it was, even if the act is not strictly justifiable by law.
    `the defender did not know or have reason to believe that the person doing the act was such public servant`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defender NOT know, and have no reason to believe (s 26), that the person doing the act was such a public servant (s 106A Explanation 1)?
    `the act is not done or attempted by the direction of a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of the public servant's office`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it the case that the act was NOT done or attempted by the direction of a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office (s 106A(2))? Answer FALSE if it was, even if the direction is not strictly justifiable by law.
    `the defender did not know or have reason to believe that the person doing the act was acting by such direction`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the defender NOT know, and have no reason to believe, that the person doing the act was acting by such direction (s 106A Explanation 2)?
    `the person doing the act did not state the authority under which he acted`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person doing the act NOT state the authority under which he acted (s 106A Explanation 2)?
    `the person doing the act had authority in writing and did not produce it when it was demanded`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person doing the act have authority in writing, which was demanded and which he did not produce (s 106A Explanation 2)?

§§ `s 96 - Nothing done in private defence is an offence`

-- 96. Nothing is an offence which is done in the exercise of the right of
-- private defence.
@export Whether an act falls within the general exception in section 96 of the Penal Code 1871 (nothing done in private defence is an offence)
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The act defended against, the act of defence, and the circumstances of both
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `exception under s 96` f IF
        "Nothing is an offence which is done in the exercise of the right of private defence"
    AND f's `the act was done in defence against the act giving rise to the right`
    AND (    `the right of private defence of the body extends to the act` f
         OR  `the right of private defence of property extends to the act` f )

§§ `s 97 - Right of private defence of the body and of property`

-- 97. Every person has a right, subject to the restrictions contained in
-- sections 98 and 106A, to defend -
--   (a) his own body, and the body of any other person, against any offence
--       affecting the human body;
--   (b) the property, whether movable or immovable, of himself or of any other
--       person, against any act which is an offence falling under the definition
--       of theft, robbery, mischief or criminal trespass, or which is an attempt
--       to commit theft, robbery, mischief or criminal trespass.
--
-- The two limbs are the two ladders below, which also carry s 99 (an act that
-- would be the offence but for the doer's condition), the start and
-- continuance of each right (ss 101, 104), and its extent (ss 100, 102, 103,
-- 105, 106). The restrictions of ss 98 and 106A are their own ladders.

@export Whether the act defended against gives rise to the right of private defence as an offence, or as an act s 99 treats as one (ss 97 and 99 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The act defended against
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the act is, or is to be treated as, an offence within sections 97 and 99` f IF
        "(s 97) against any offence"
    ..  f's `the act defended against is an offence`
    OR  "(s 99) When an act, which would otherwise be a certain offence, is not that offence, by reason of"
    ..  f's `the act would be that offence but for the youth, want of maturity of understanding, unsoundness of mind or intoxication of the person doing it, or any misconception on his part`

@export Whether the right of private defence of the body extends to the act of defence (ss 97(a), 98, 99, 100, 101, 102, 103 and 106A of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The act defended against, the act of defence, and the circumstances of both
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of the body extends to the act` f IF
        "97. Every person has a right, subject to the restrictions contained in sections 98 and 106A, to defend - (a) his own body, and the body of any other person, against"
    AND f's `the act falls under the definition of an offence affecting the human body, or is an attempt or a threat to commit one`
    AND `the act is, or is to be treated as, an offence within sections 97 and 99` f
    AND "101.-(1) The right of private defence of the body starts as soon as"
          ... f's `the defender reasonably believed that there was danger to the body, his own or that of any other person, arising from that act`
    AND "(2) The right of private defence continues as long as"
          ... f's `that belief of danger continued when the act of defence was done`
    AND (    `the right of private defence of the body extends to the harm caused to the assailant within sections 102 and 103` f
         OR  `the right of private defence extends to the running of the risk within section 100` f )
    AND `within the restrictions of section 98` f
    AND `section 106A does not exclude the right` f

@export Whether the right of private defence of property extends to the act of defence (ss 97(b), 98, 99, 104, 105, 106 and 106A of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The act defended against, the act of defence, and the circumstances of both
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property extends to the act` f IF
        "97. Every person has a right, subject to the restrictions contained in sections 98 and 106A, to defend - (b) the property, whether movable or immovable, of himself or of any other person, against any act which is an offence falling under the definition of"
    AND (    f's `the act falls under the definition of theft, or is an attempt to commit theft`
         OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of robbery, or is an attempt to commit robbery`
         OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of mischief, or is an attempt to commit mischief`
         OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of criminal trespass, or is an attempt to commit criminal trespass` )
    AND `the act is, or is to be treated as, an offence within sections 97 and 99` f
    AND "104.-(1) The right of private defence of property starts when"
          ... f's `the defender reasonably believed that there was a danger to property, his own or that of any other person, arising from that act`
    AND `the right of private defence of property continues within section 104` f
    AND `the right of private defence of property extends to the harm caused to the wrongdoer within sections 105 and 106` f
    AND `within the restrictions of section 98` f
    AND `section 106A does not exclude the right` f

§§ `s 98 - Extent to which right may be exercised`

-- 98.-(1) The right of private defence does not extend to the inflicting of
-- more harm than it is reasonably necessary in the circumstances.
-- (2) There is no right of private defence in cases in which there is
-- reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public
-- authority in the circumstances.
@export Whether an act of private defence is within the restrictions of section 98 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The harm inflicted, and whether a public authority could have been called on
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `within the restrictions of section 98` f IF
        "(1) The right of private defence does not extend to the inflicting of more harm than it is reasonably necessary in the circumstances"
    AND f's `the harm inflicted was no more than it was reasonably necessary to inflict in the circumstances`
    AND "(2) There is no right of private defence in cases in which there is reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public authority in the circumstances"
          ... f's `there was no reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public authority in the circumstances`

§§ `s 99 - Right of private defence against act of person of unsound mind, etc.`

-- 99. When an act, which would otherwise be a certain offence, is not that
-- offence, by reason of the youth, the want of maturity of understanding, the
-- unsoundness of mind, or the intoxication of the person doing that act, or by
-- reason of any misconception on the part of that person, every person has the
-- same right of private defence against that act which he would have if the
-- act were that offence.
--
-- Encoded as the second limb of `the act is, or is to be treated as, an offence
-- within sections 97 and 99` (under s 97 above): the act's own description
-- (theft, an offence affecting the body ...) is unchanged, and s 99 supplies
-- the "is an offence" that the doer's condition took away.

§§ `s 100 - Right of private defence against deadly assault when there is risk of harm to innocent person`

-- 100. If, in the exercise of the right of private defence against an assault
-- which causes the defender to reasonably believe that death would be caused to
-- him or to any other person and the defender is so situated that he cannot
-- effectually exercise that right without risk of harm to an innocent person,
-- his right of private defence extends to the running of that risk.
@export Whether the right of private defence extends to the running of a risk of harm to an innocent person, under section 100 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The assault, and the risk to innocent persons
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence extends to the running of the risk within section 100` f IF
        f's `the harm was caused to an innocent person, in running a risk of harm to an innocent person`
    AND "If, in the exercise of the right of private defence against"
          ... f's `an assault which causes the defender to reasonably believe that death would be caused to him or to any other person`
    AND "and"
          ... f's `the defender is so situated that he cannot effectually exercise that right without risk of harm to an innocent person`
    AND "his right of private defence extends to the running of that risk"

§§ `s 101 - Start and continuance of right of private defence of body`

-- 101.-(1) The right of private defence of the body starts as soon as the
-- defender reasonably believes that there is danger to the body (either his own
-- or that of any other person) arising from any act which is an offence against
-- the human body or an attempt or a threat to commit the offence, though the
-- offence may not have been committed.
-- (2) The right of private defence continues as long as the belief of danger
-- mentioned in subsection (1) continues.
--
-- Encoded in `the right of private defence of the body extends to the act`.

§§ `ss 102 and 103 - How far the right of private defence of the body extends`

-- 102. The right of private defence of the body extends, under the
-- restrictions mentioned in sections 98 and 106A, to the voluntary causing of
-- death to the assailant, if the offence which gives rise to the exercise of the
-- right is of any of the following descriptions:
--   (a) an assault where the defender reasonably believes that death will
--       otherwise be the consequence of such assault;
--   (b) an assault where the defender reasonably believes that grievous hurt
--       will otherwise be the consequence of such assault;
--   (c) an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the
--       intention of committing rape as described in section 375 or causing
--       such rape to be committed;
--   (d) an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the
--       intention of causing penile penetration of the vagina, anus or mouth as
--       described in section 376(2);
--   (e) an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the
--       intention of kidnapping or abducting;
--   (f) an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the
--       intention of wrongfully confining a person, under circumstances which
--       may reasonably cause him to believe that he will be unable to have
--       opportunity for recourse to a public authority for his release.
--
-- 103. If the offence is not of any of the descriptions mentioned in section
-- 102, the right of private defence of the body does not extend to the
-- voluntary causing of death to the assailant, but does extend, under the
-- restrictions mentioned in sections 98 and 106A, to the voluntary causing to
-- the assailant of any harm other than death.
--
-- Where s 102 lets the right reach death, it reaches every lesser harm too; so
-- the extent is "harm other than death" (s 103) OR a s 102 description.
@export Whether the right of private defence of the body extends to the harm the defender caused to the assailant, under sections 102 and 103 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The assault, and the harm caused to the assailant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of the body extends to the harm caused to the assailant within sections 102 and 103` f IF
        "103. ... does extend ... to the voluntary causing to the assailant of any harm other than death"
    ..  f's `the harm voluntarily caused to the assailant was harm other than death`
    OR  "102. The right of private defence of the body extends ... to the voluntary causing of death to the assailant, if the offence which gives rise to the exercise of the right is of any of the following descriptions: (a)"
    ..  f's `an assault where the defender reasonably believes that death will otherwise be the consequence of such assault`
    ..  "(b)"
    ..  f's `an assault where the defender reasonably believes that grievous hurt will otherwise be the consequence of such assault`
    ..  "(c)"
    ..  f's `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of committing rape as described in section 375 or causing such rape to be committed`
    ..  "(d)"
    ..  f's `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of causing penile penetration of the vagina, anus or mouth as described in section 376(2)`
    ..  "(e)"
    ..  f's `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of kidnapping or abducting`
    ..  "(f)"
    ..  f's `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of wrongfully confining a person, under circumstances which may reasonably cause him to believe that he will be unable to have opportunity for recourse to a public authority for his release`

§§ `s 104 - Commencement and continuance of right of private defence of property`

-- 104.-(1) The right of private defence of property starts when the defender
-- reasonably believes that there was a danger to property (either his own or
-- that of any other person) arising from any act which is an offence falling
-- under the definition of theft, robbery, mischief or criminal trespass, or
-- which is an attempt to commit such an offence.
-- (2) The right of private defence of property against theft continues till -
--   (a) the offender has effected his retreat with the property;
--   (b) the assistance of a public authority is obtained; or
--   (c) the property has been recovered.
-- (3) The right of private defence of property against robbery continues as
-- long as the offender causes or attempts to cause to any person death or hurt
-- or wrongful restraint, or as long as the fear of instant death or of instant
-- hurt or of instant personal restraint continues.
-- (4) The right of private defence of property against criminal trespass or
-- mischief continues as long as the offender continues in the commission of
-- criminal trespass or mischief.
-- (5) The right of private defence of property against house-breaking continues
-- as long as such house-breaking continues.
--
-- (1) is in `the right of private defence of property extends to the act`;
-- (2)-(5) are below. Each limb pairs the offence with its own continuance, so
-- that the right continues if it continues against ANY of the offences the act
-- falls under (a robbery is also a theft: FORK E-7).
@export Whether the right of private defence of property still continued when the act of defence was done, under section 104(2)-(5) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The offence against property, and how far it had gone
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property continues within section 104` f IF
        (    "(2) The right of private defence of property against"
         AND f's `the act falls under the definition of theft, or is an attempt to commit theft`
         AND "continues till - (a)"
               ... f's `the offender had not effected his retreat with the property`
               ... "(b)"
               ... f's `the assistance of a public authority had not been obtained`
               ... "(c)"
               ... f's `the property had not been recovered` )
    OR  (    "(3) The right of private defence of property against"
         AND f's `the act falls under the definition of robbery, or is an attempt to commit robbery`
         AND "continues as long as"
               ... (    f's `the offender was causing or attempting to cause to any person death or hurt or wrongful restraint`
                  OR  f's `the fear of instant death or of instant hurt or of instant personal restraint continued` ) )
    OR  (    "(4) The right of private defence of property against"
         AND (    f's `the act falls under the definition of criminal trespass, or is an attempt to commit criminal trespass`
              OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of mischief, or is an attempt to commit mischief` )
         AND "continues as long as"
               ... f's `the offender continued in the commission of the criminal trespass or mischief` )
    OR  (    "(5) The right of private defence of property against"
         AND f's `the criminal trespass is house-breaking`
         AND "continues as long as"
               ... f's `the house-breaking continued` )

§§ `ss 105 and 106 - How far the right of private defence of property extends`

-- 105.-(1) The right of private defence of property extends, under the
-- restrictions mentioned in sections 98 and 106A, to the voluntary causing of
-- death to the wrongdoer when the defender reasonably believes that there was a
-- danger to property (either his own or that of any other person) arising from
-- any of the following descriptions:
--   (a) robbery;
--   (b) house-breaking committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m. and if the
--       wrongdoer -
--         (i) effects his entrance into the house or any part of it in any of
--             the 6 ways described in subsection (2); or
--         (ii) being in the house or any part of it for the purpose of
--             committing an offence, or having committed an offence therein,
--             the wrongdoer leaves the house or any part of it in any of these
--             6 ways;
--   (c) mischief by fire committed on any building, tent, container or vessel,
--       which building, tent, container or vessel is used as a human dwelling,
--       or as a place for the custody of property;
--   (d) theft, mischief or house-breaking, under such circumstances where the
--       defender reasonably believes that death or grievous hurt will be the
--       consequence, if such right of private defence is not exercised.
-- (2) For the purposes of subsection (1)(b), the 6 ways of entry or leaving by
-- a wrongdoer are as follows: [(a)-(f), quoted at the ladder below]
--
-- 106. If the offence, the committing or the attempt to commit of which
-- occasions the exercise of the right of private defence, is theft, mischief,
-- or criminal trespass, not of any of the descriptions mentioned in section
-- 105, that right does not extend to the voluntary causing of death, but does
-- extend, subject to the restrictions mentioned in sections 98 and 106A, to the
-- voluntary causing to the wrongdoer of any harm other than death.

@export Whether a time of day (in minutes after midnight) is "after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m." (s 105(1)(b) of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN `minutes after midnight` IS A NUMBER @desc The time of the house-breaking, in minutes after midnight - for example 1170 for 7.30 p.m.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m.` `minutes after midnight` IF
        `minutes after midnight` > 1140
    OR  `minutes after midnight` < 420

@export Whether a wrongdoer entered or left in one of the 6 ways described in section 105(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc How the wrongdoer entered or left the house
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `in one of the 6 ways described in section 105(2)` f IF
        "(2) For the purposes of subsection (1)(b), the 6 ways of entry or leaving by a wrongdoer are as follows: (a)"
    ..  f's `enters or leaves through a passage made by the wrongdoer, or by any abettor of the house-breaking, in order to commit the house-breaking`
    ..  "(b)"
    ..  f's `enters or leaves through a passage not intended for human entrance, or one reached by scaling or climbing over any wall or building`
    ..  "(c)"
    ..  f's `enters or leaves through a passage opened, in order to commit the house-breaking, by means the occupier did not intend it to be opened by`
    ..  "(d)"
    ..  f's `enters or leaves by opening any lock in order to commit the house-breaking, or to leave after it`
    ..  "(e)"
    ..  f's `effects his entry or leaving by using criminal force or committing an assault, or by threatening any person with assault`
    ..  "(f)"
    ..  f's `enters or leaves by a passage he knows to have been fastened against it, and to have been unfastened by himself or an abettor`

@export Whether the right of private defence of property extends to the voluntary causing of death to the wrongdoer, under section 105(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The offence against property, and the danger the defender believed in
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property extends to causing death within section 105` f IF
        "(1) The right of private defence of property extends ... to the voluntary causing of death to the wrongdoer when the defender reasonably believes that there was a danger to property ... arising from any of the following descriptions: (a)"
    AND (    f's `the act falls under the definition of robbery, or is an attempt to commit robbery`
         OR  (    "(b)"
              AND f's `the criminal trespass is house-breaking`
              AND f's `the house-breaking was committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m.`
              AND "and if the wrongdoer"
                    ... (    "(i)"
                       OR  f's `the wrongdoer effected his entrance into the house or any part of it`
                       OR  "(ii)"
                       OR  f's `the wrongdoer, being in the house for the purpose of committing an offence or having committed an offence therein, leaves the house or any part of it` )
              AND "in any of the 6 ways described in subsection (2)"
                    ... `in one of the 6 ways described in section 105(2)` f )
         OR  (    "(c)"
                ... f's `the mischief is mischief by fire committed on a building, tent, container or vessel used as a human dwelling, or as a place for the custody of property` )
         OR  (    "(d)"
              AND (    f's `the act falls under the definition of theft, or is an attempt to commit theft`
                   OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of mischief, or is an attempt to commit mischief`
                   OR  f's `the criminal trespass is house-breaking` )
              AND "under such circumstances where"
                    ... f's `the defender reasonably believes that death or grievous hurt will be the consequence, if such right of private defence is not exercised` ) )

@export Whether the right of private defence of property extends to the harm the defender caused to the wrongdoer, under sections 105 and 106 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc The offence against property, and the harm caused to the wrongdoer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right of private defence of property extends to the harm caused to the wrongdoer within sections 105 and 106` f IF
        "105"
    ..  `the right of private defence of property extends to causing death within section 105` f
    OR  (    "106. If the offence ... is"
         AND (    f's `the act falls under the definition of theft, or is an attempt to commit theft`
              OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of mischief, or is an attempt to commit mischief`
              OR  f's `the act falls under the definition of criminal trespass, or is an attempt to commit criminal trespass` )
         AND "not of any of the descriptions mentioned in section 105, that right does not extend to the voluntary causing of death, but does extend ... to the voluntary causing to the wrongdoer of"
               ... f's `the harm voluntarily caused to the wrongdoer was harm other than death` )

§§ `s 106A - Acts against which there is no right of private defence`

-- 106A.-(1) There is no right of private defence against an act which does not
-- cause the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would
-- result, if done, or attempted to be done, by a public servant acting in good
-- faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office, though that act
-- may not be strictly justifiable by law.
-- (2) There is no right of private defence against an act which does not cause
-- the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result,
-- if done, or attempted to be done, by the direction of a public servant acting
-- in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of the public servant's
-- office, though that direction may not be strictly justifiable by law.
-- Explanation 1.-A person is not deprived of the right of private defence
-- against an act done, or attempted to be done, by a public servant, as such,
-- unless he knows, or has reason to believe, that the person doing the act is
-- such public servant.
-- Explanation 2.-A person (A) is not deprived of the right of private defence
-- against an act done, or attempted to be done, by the direction of a public
-- servant, unless A knows, or has reason to believe, that the person doing the
-- act (B) is acting by such direction; or unless B states the authority under
-- which B acts, or, if B has authority in writing, unless B produces such
-- authority, if demanded.
--
-- Written as the case in which s 106A does NOT take the right away, in the
-- defender's words (FORK E-1). Explanation 2's last clause is read as: where
-- B has written authority and it is demanded, stating it is not enough - B must
-- produce it (FORK E-8).
@export Whether section 106A of the Penal Code 1871 leaves the right of private defence in place (it removes it against a public servant, or one acting by his direction, acting in good faith under colour of office)
GIVEN f IS A `Private Defence Facts` @desc Who did the act defended against, under whose authority, and what the defender knew
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 106A does not exclude the right` f IF
        "There is no right of private defence against an act which does not cause the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result"
    ..  f's `the act causes the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result`
    OR  (    "(1) ... if done, or attempted to be done, by a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office"
         AND (    f's `the act is not done or attempted by a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office`
              OR  "Explanation 1.-A person is not deprived of the right of private defence ... unless he knows, or has reason to believe, that the person doing the act is such public servant"
              OR  f's `the defender did not know or have reason to believe that the person doing the act was such public servant` )
         AND "(2) ... if done, or attempted to be done, by the direction of a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of the public servant's office"
         AND (    f's `the act is not done or attempted by the direction of a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of the public servant's office`
              OR  (    "Explanation 2.-A person (A) is not deprived of the right of private defence ... unless A knows, or has reason to believe, that the person doing the act (B) is acting by such direction"
                   AND f's `the defender did not know or have reason to believe that the person doing the act was acting by such direction`
                   AND "or unless B states the authority under which B acts, or, if B has authority in writing, unless B produces such authority, if demanded"
                   AND (    f's `the person doing the act did not state the authority under which he acted`
                        OR  f's `the person doing the act had authority in writing and did not produce it when it was demanded` ) ) ) )
